[[{"text": "HSC 2 Unit ATAR Notes", "x0": 0.11871657754010695, "y0": 0.09256198347107437, "x1": 0.5454545454545454, "y1": 0.10165289256198347}, {"text": "Probability", "x0": 0.8032085561497326, "y0": 0.09256198347107437, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.10413223140495868}, {"text": "Tntroduction. An important concept in mathematical statistics is the concept of \"probability\u201d.", "x0": 0.14224598930481283, "y0": 0.10909090909090909, "x1": 0.8342245989304813, "y1": 0.1206611570247934}, {"text": "How", "x0": 0.8513368983957219, "y0": 0.10909090909090909, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.11818181818181818}, {"text": "likely are we to get the results that we want, and how many tries should we take to definitely", "x0": 0.11871657754010695, "y0": 0.12479338842975207, "x1": 0.8096256684491978, "y1": 0.13636363636363635}, {"text": "get those", "x0": 0.8171122994652407, "y0": 0.12479338842975207, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.13636363636363635}, {"text": "results? Unfortunately in the HSC, we won't be covering those later applications, however an", "x0": 0.11871657754010695, "y0": 0.1396694214876033, "x1": 0.788235294117647, "y1": 0.15123966942148762}, {"text": "introduction", "x0": 0.7957219251336899, "y0": 0.1396694214876033, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.1487603305785124}, {"text": "to the field is covered. Later, if you go on to a degree in commerce, science or engineering,", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.15454545454545454, "x1": 0.7796791443850267, "y1": 0.16611570247933885}, {"text": "you may find", "x0": 0.788235294117647, "y0": 0.15454545454545454, "x1": 0.881283422459893, "y1": 0.16611570247933885}, {"text": "yourself happy we took these first few steps.", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.17024793388429751, "x1": 0.42780748663101603, "y1": 0.18181818181818182}, {"text": "Defining a Probability Space. Before we can go into extensive applications of probability,", "x0": 0.14224598930481283, "y0": 0.18512396694214875, "x1": 0.8074866310160428, "y1": 0.19669421487603306}, {"text": "or how to", "x0": 0.8149732620320855, "y0": 0.18512396694214875, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.19421487603305784}, {"text": "compute a probability, it is important we understand how we define a probability.", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.2, "x1": 0.6983957219251337, "y1": 0.2115702479338843}, {"text": "Consider the simple case of the coin - when you flip, either two things will happen. Either", "x0": 0.14224598930481283, "y0": 0.21487603305785125, "x1": 0.7700534759358288, "y1": 0.22644628099173553}, {"text": "you get heads,", "x0": 0.7818181818181819, "y0": 0.21487603305785125, "x1": 0.881283422459893, "y1": 0.22644628099173553}, {"text": "or tails. So, in total, two things can happen, and you can get either one equally. Common sense", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.23057851239669422, "x1": 0.7935828877005348, "y1": 0.2421487603305785}, {"text": "tells us that", "x0": 0.7989304812834225, "y0": 0.23057851239669422, "x1": 0.881283422459893, "y1": 0.2396694214876033}, {"text": "we have a 50% chance of getting a heads or tails. This leads to the definition of a probability.", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.24462809917355371, "x1": 0.7828877005347593, "y1": 0.25702479338842976}, {"text": "Since each event is equally likely, the probability of a single event is defined to be +, where", "x0": 0.14224598930481283, "y0": 0.25867768595041324, "x1": 0.8181818181818182, "y1": 0.2735537190082645}, {"text": "n is the", "x0": 0.825668449197861, "y0": 0.2603305785123967, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.2694214876033058}, {"text": "number of outcomes. In this case, there are two equally likely outcomes, so the probability", "x0": 0.11871657754010695, "y0": 0.27520661157024795, "x1": 0.7967914438502673, "y1": 0.28677685950413223}, {"text": "of any one", "x0": 0.8053475935828877, "y0": 0.27520661157024795, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.28677685950413223}, {"text": "occuring is 4. Similarly, with a die, there are 6 equally likely events, so the probability of any", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.2909090909090909, "x1": 0.786096256684492, "y1": 0.30413223140495865}, {"text": "one occuring", "x0": 0.7925133689839572, "y0": 0.29421487603305785, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.30247933884297523}, {"text": "\"Mutually Exclusive Outcomes. Of course, after considering the die, we naturally ask", "x0": 0.13689839572192514, "y0": 0.31322314049586775, "x1": 0.7989304812834225, "y1": 0.3322314049586777}, {"text": "ourselves -", "x0": 0.8064171122994652, "y0": 0.32066115702479336, "x1": 0.881283422459893, "y1": 0.32975206611570246}, {"text": "what's the probability of not just getting one number, but multiple? For example, what if", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.33553719008264465, "x1": 0.7775401069518717, "y1": 0.34710743801652894}, {"text": "we wanted an", "x0": 0.7839572192513369, "y0": 0.33553719008264465, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.3446280991735537}, {"text": "even number?", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.3512396694214876, "x1": 0.21497326203208555, "y1": 0.3603305785123967}, {"text": "Well, there are 3 even numbers, and 6 total numbe", "x0": 0.14224598930481283, "y0": 0.36611570247933883, "x1": 0.5144385026737968, "y1": 0.3768595041322314}, {"text": "the probability of getting an even", "x0": 0.560427807486631, "y0": 0.36611570247933883, "x1": 0.8042780748663102, "y1": 0.3776859504132231}, {"text": "number is", "x0": 0.8117647058823529, "y0": 0.36611570247933883, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.3752066115702479}, {"text": "However, we could also see this as getting a 2, 4 or 6. This way, the probability of an", "x0": 0.17326203208556148, "y0": 0.38099173553719007, "x1": 0.7850267379679144, "y1": 0.3925619834710744}, {"text": "even number", "x0": 0.7914438502673797, "y0": 0.38099173553719007, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.39008264462809916}, {"text": "is the sum of these events. We could write P(even number) = P(2) + F(4) +", "x0": 0.12299465240641712, "y0": 0.3884297520661157, "x1": 0.6577540106951871, "y1": 0.4140495867768595}, {"text": "*But the first method was so much easier and quicker!\u201d you cry.", "x0": 0.14224598930481283, "y0": 0.4033057851239669, "x1": 0.5957219251336898, "y1": 0.4289256198347107}, {"text": "\u00e9", "x0": 0.8096256684491978, "y0": 0.4041322314049587, "x1": 0.8149732620320855, "y1": 0.4099173553719008}, {"text": "2", "x0": 0.841711229946524, "y0": 0.4041322314049587, "x1": 0.8470588235294118, "y1": 0.4099173553719008}, {"text": "hould I worry about", "x0": 0.66524064171123, "y0": 0.4115702479338843, "x1": 0.8032085561497326, "y1": 0.4231404958677686}, {"text": "something", "x0": 0.8106951871657754, "y0": 0.4115702479338843, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.4231404958677686}, {"text": "harder and with more work?!\u201d you ask? The answer is simple - this allows us to define addition", "x0": 0.11871657754010695, "y0": 0.42644628099173554, "x1": 0.7989304812834225, "y1": 0.4380165289256198}, {"text": "in terms of", "x0": 0.8053475935828877, "y0": 0.42644628099173554, "x1": 0.8834224598930481, "y1": 0.43553719008264463}, {"text": "probabilitis", "x0": 0.11871657754010695, "y0": 0.4413223140495868, "x1": 0.19144385026737967, "y1": 0.45289256198347105}, {"text": "Before we considered simple cases, however what about something a little more difficult?", "x0": 0.14224598930481283, "y0": 0.4570247933884298, "x1": 0.7839572192513369, "y1": 0.46859504132231405}, {"text": "Let\u2019s say", "x0": 0.7946524064171123, "y0": 0.4570247933884298, "x1": 0.8524064171122995, "y1": 0.46859504132231405}, {"text": "yy we", "x0": 0.8491978609625669, "y0": 0.46033057851239667, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.4669421487603306}, {"text": "have a die that\u2019s weighted such that the number on each side is that much more likely to be", "x0": 0.11871657754010695, "y0": 0.4636363636363636, "x1": 0.7807486631016043, "y1": 0.48925619834710743}, {"text": "attained. For", "x0": 0.788235294117647, "y0": 0.471900826446281, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.4809917355371901}, {"text": "example, a 6 is 6x more likely than a 1, and 6/2=3x more likely than a 2. This defines a relative", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.47851239669421486, "x1": 0.7957219251336899, "y1": 0.5041322314049587}, {"text": "probability,", "x0": 0.8021390374331551, "y0": 0.48677685950413224, "x1": 0.881283422459893, "y1": 0.4983471074380165}, {"text": "where p(2) = and so on, but what about an absolute probability?", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.4933884297520661, "x1": 0.6267379679144385, "y1": 0.5190082644628099}, {"text": "From an earlier definition, we know that all probabilities are ont of 1. Using addition as", "x0": 0.14224598930481283, "y0": 0.5173553719008265, "x1": 0.7839572192513369, "y1": 0.5289256198347108}, {"text": "we defined it", "x0": 0.7903743315508022, "y0": 0.5173553719008265, "x1": 0.881283422459893, "y1": 0.5264462809917355}, {"text": "carlier, if we add up all mutually exclusive outcomes, they will come to 1, However, what", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.5322314049586777, "x1": 0.7871657754010695, "y1": 0.543801652892562}, {"text": "do we define", "x0": 0.7946524064171123, "y0": 0.5322314049586777, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.5413223140495868}, {"text": "as mutually exclusive? Well, two events are mutually exclusive if they cannot occur together.", "x0": 0.11871657754010695, "y0": 0.547107438016529, "x1": 0.8053475935828877, "y1": 0.5586776859504132}, {"text": "A simple", "x0": 0.8192513368983957, "y0": 0.547107438016529, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.5586776859504132}, {"text": "scenario is getting heads AND tails from a single coin toss. Or, getting an even AND an odd", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.5619834710743802, "x1": 0.7850267379679144, "y1": 0.5735537190082645}, {"text": "number on a", "x0": 0.7925133689839572, "y0": 0.5619834710743802, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.5710743801652892}, {"text": "die, An example of two events that aren\u2019t mutaully exclusive are rolling that die and getting", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.5776859504132231, "x1": 0.8, "y1": 0.5892561983471074}, {"text": "a 1 and an", "x0": 0.8064171122994652, "y0": 0.5776859504132231, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.5867768595041323}, {"text": "odd number:", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.5925619834710744, "x1": 0.20320855614973263, "y1": 0.6016528925619835}, {"text": "So, back to those absolute probabilities. If we add up a set of mutually exclusive events, they", "x0": 0.14224598930481283, "y0": 0.6074380165289256, "x1": 0.8010695187165775, "y1": 0.6190082644628099}, {"text": "must come", "x0": 0.8074866310160428, "y0": 0.6090909090909091, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.6165289256198347}, {"text": "to 1. So, let\u2019s consider the obvious set of mutually exclusive events - getting a 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or", "x0": 0.11764705882352941, "y0": 0.6223140495867768, "x1": 0.7850267379679144, "y1": 0.6338842975206611}, {"text": "6. Obviously", "x0": 0.7914438502673797, "y0": 0.6223140495867768, "x1": 0.8823529411764706, "y1": 0.6338842975206611}, {"text": "if I get a2, I can\u2019t also get a 3. So, this gives us the following mathematical solving:", "x0": 0.11871657754010695, "y0": 0.6380165289256199, "x1": 0.7155080213903743, "y1": 0.6495867768595042}, {"text": "P(Q) = P(1) + P(2) + P(3) + P(4) + P(5) + PG)", "x0": 0.31016042780748665, "y0": 0.6768595041322314, "x1": 0.6481283422459893, "y1": 0.6892561983471074}, {"text": "= P(1) + 2P(1) + 3F(1) + 4P(1) + 5P(1) + 6P(1)", "x0": 0.3497326203208556, "y0": 0.6958677685950413, "x1": 0.6887700534759358, "y1": 0.7082644628099174}, {"text": "=1", "x0": 0.41818181818181815, "y0": 0.7148760330578512, "x1": 0.4406417112299465, "y1": 0.7231404958677686}, {"text": "1", "x0": 0.42459893048128344, "y0": 0.7330578512396694, "x1": 0.4288770053475936, "y1": 0.7413223140495868}, {"text": "=o", "x0": 0.3893048128342246, "y0": 0.740495867768595, "x1": 0.43422459893048126, "y1": 0.7586776859504132}, {"text": "Summary Notes. Finally, it\u2019s important to summarise some important concepts defined", "x0": 0.14331550802139037, "y0": 0.7677685950413223, "x1": 0.7796791443850267, "y1": 0.7793388429752066}, {"text": "through thes", "x0": 0.7850267379679144, "y0": 0.7677685950413223, "x1": 0.8748663101604278, "y1": 0.7793388429752066}, {"text": "notes. You will probably (all puns intended) find these formula useful to contain on any summary", "x0": 0.11871657754010695, "y0": 0.7826446280991736, "x1": 0.8160427807486631, "y1": 0.7950413223140496}, {"text": "sheet:", "x0": 0.8224598930481284, "y0": 0.7826446280991736, "x1": 0.8577540106951872, "y1": 0.7917355371900826}, {"text": "For any event A, 0 < P(A) <1", "x0": 0.15935828877005348, "y0": 0.8049586776859504, "x1": 0.3764705882352941, "y1": 0.8165289256198347}, {"text": "number of inf", "x0": 0.5187165775401069, "y0": 0.8264462809917356, "x1": 0.6802139037433155, "y1": 0.8330578512396695}, {"text": "of events", "x0": 0.5850267379679145, "y0": 0.8264462809917356, "x1": 0.6331550802139038, "y1": 0.8495867768595041}, {"text": "For any event B in some sample space 9, P(E) =", "x0": 0.15935828877005348, "y0": 0.8289256198347107, "x1": 0.5112299465240642, "y1": 0.8404958677685951}, {"text": "\u00a9 For any set of mutually exclusive events \u00a31, B2,...,En, 0\", = 1 and P(E) U...U", "x0": 0.14224598930481283, "y0": 0.8462809917355372, "x1": 0.8235294117647058, "y1": 0.868595041322314}, {"text": "Em) =", "x0": 0.8310160427807487, "y0": 0.8520661157024794, "x1": 0.881283422459893, "y1": 0.8644628099173554}, {"text": "+... + P(Em)", "x0": 0.2053475935828877, "y0": 0.8669421487603306, "x1": 0.2994652406417112, "y1": 0.8793388429752066}]]